\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Nested paired eliminations compose}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Let #{V=R\oplus F\oplus E} be a direct sum of finite-dimensional complex vector spaces, and let #{K:V\to V}. Write #{D_E} for the compression of #{K} to #{E}, and assume that #{D_E} is invertible. Let #{S'} be the Schur complement of that compression, on #{R\oplus F}, and assume that the compression #{D_F} of #{S'} to #{F} is invertible. Let #{S} be the Schur complement of #{D_F} in #{S'}. No positivity or adjoint relation is assumed. On every element of the paired algebra,
##{\mathcal E_R^{S'}\circ\mathcal E_{R\oplus F}^{K}=\mathcal E_R^{K},}
where each symbol denotes the \vocabk{normalized paired conditional functional}{fdip-00L5} of its indicated precision and eliminated summand.}

\proof{
\p{The canonical quadratic element splits as a sum of the three pairwise coevaluations. Those summands are even and commute. In the multinomial expansion of #{(\theta_R+\theta_F+\theta_E)^{\dim V}/(\dim V)!}, the only surviving top term takes each summand to its own dimension, and that term is #{\Omega_R\Omega_F\Omega_E}. Hence
##{\Omega_V=\Omega_R\Omega_F\Omega_E.}
The identity #{J_F(X\Omega_F)=X} therefore gives #{J_FJ_E=J_{F\oplus E}} on every exterior monomial.}

\p{Order the eliminated basis as #{F} then #{E}, and write the compression of #{K} to #{F\oplus E} as
##{\begin{pmatrix}P&Q\\ N&D_E\end{pmatrix}.}
Its determinant equals #{\det(D_E)\det(P-QD_E^{-1}N)}. The block #{P-QD_E^{-1}N} is the compression of #{S'} to #{F}, namely #{D_F}. Thus the combined compression is invertible and
##{\det(K_{F\oplus E})=\det(D_E)\det(D_F).}
This is the block-determinant identity recorded for the [moment theorem](fdip-008N).}

\p{Let #{w=e^{\theta_K}}. The partial formula gives
##{z=J_E(w)=\det(D_E)\,e^{\theta_{S'}},}
an even central unit of the paired algebra on #{R\oplus F}. Applying #{J_F} yields
##{t=J_F(z)=\det(D_E)\det(D_F)\,e^{\theta_S},}
an even central unit of the paired algebra on #{R}. The raw identity #{J_{F\oplus E}=J_FJ_E} is the hypothesis #{D=Q\circ P} of \ref{fdip-00KX}, with this weight #{w}. Its inner normalized map is #{\mathcal E_{R\oplus F}^{K}}. Its outer map multiplies by the constant #{\det(D_E)} and then divides by #{t}, which cancels that constant and leaves #{\mathcal E_R^{S'}}.}

\p{It remains to match the direct map with #{\mathcal E_R^{K}}. Order the basis as #{R}, then #{F}, then #{E}, and write
##{K=\begin{pmatrix}
A&B_F&B_E\\
C_F&P&Q\\
C_E&N&D_E
\end{pmatrix}.}
The Schur complement #{S'} of #{D_E} has blocks
##{\begin{aligned}
A'&=A-B_ED_E^{-1}C_E,\\
B'&=B_F-B_ED_E^{-1}N,\\
C'&=C_F-QD_E^{-1}C_E,
\end{aligned}}
and #{F}-block #{D_F}. The complement of #{D_F} in #{S'} is #{S=A'-B'D_F^{-1}C'}. The inverse of the combined compression is
##{\begin{pmatrix}
D_F^{-1}&-D_F^{-1}QD_E^{-1}\\
-D_E^{-1}ND_F^{-1}&D_E^{-1}+D_E^{-1}ND_F^{-1}QD_E^{-1}
\end{pmatrix}.}
The first block row of this inverse, applied to the column #{(C_F,C_E)}, equals #{D_F^{-1}C'}. The second block row, applied to that column, equals #{D_E^{-1}C_E-D_E^{-1}ND_F^{-1}C'}. Multiplying on the left by #{(B_F,B_E)} therefore produces
##{B_ED_E^{-1}C_E+(B_F-B_ED_E^{-1}N)D_F^{-1}C'
=B_ED_E^{-1}C_E+B'D_F^{-1}C'.}
Subtracting that product from #{A} leaves #{A'-B'D_F^{-1}C'=S}. Thus the one-step Schur complement of #{K_{F\oplus E}} is #{S}, and the direct normalized map is #{\mathcal E_R^{K}}.}
}
